{
  "gmf_version": 1,
  "name": "Hidradenitis Suppurativa",
  "remarks": [
    "Hidradenitis suppurativa (HS), also called acne inversa, is a chronic ",
    "inflammatory skin disease of apocrine-gland-bearing skin (axillae, groin, ",
    "perianal and inframammary regions). It presents with recurrent painful ",
    "nodules that progress to abscesses, rupture and drainage, sinus tracts ",
    "and scarring. Disease severity is graded with the Hurley staging system ",
    "(I: abscesses without tracts/scarring, II: recurrent abscesses with ",
    "tracts and scarring, III: diffuse involvement with interconnected tracts).",
    "Epidemiology modeled here: prevalence roughly 1% of the population; ",
    "female to male ratio about 3:1; onset typically post-puberty, most often ",
    "in the 20s (range teens to middle age); about one third of patients ",
    "report a family history. Obesity and smoking are associated risk factors.",
    "Diagnosis is frequently delayed by several years; HS is often mistaken ",
    "for recurrent boils or folliculitis early on.",
    "First-line treatment is oral antibiotics (doxycycline, clindamycin-based ",
    "regimens). Adalimumab, a TNF-alpha inhibitor, was FDA-approved in 2015 ",
    "for moderate-to-severe HS. Severe disease may require surgical deroofing ",
    "or excision (not modeled here).",
    "Coding: SNOMED-CT 59393003 (Hidradenitis suppurativa), ICD-10-CM L73.2, ",
    "ICD-9-CM 705.83.",
    "Sources: DermNet (dermnetnz.org/topics/hidradenitis-suppurativa), MSD ",
    "Manual, Autoimmune Registry disease profile, JAAD International real-",
    "world HS phenotype study (SNOMED-CT 59393003).",
    "All patients and data generated by this module are fully synthetic."
  ],
